@LW 

Boolean algebra was the only class I ever encountered that simultaneously had engineering, philosophy, and math majors in attendance, and a different course number for each of those. (Some of the philosophy majors were traumatized by it, though.)

I would expect that most crossword solvers would love it if they gave it a chance. You can understand it without having to know calculus or algebra. It's logic reduced to its basic concepts, and its operations correspond to specific electronic components so that understanding Boolean algebra translates directly to understanding logic circuit design.

@LW

I knew the term BOOLEAN ALGEBRA, but never thought about it in terms of math, only in terms of BOOLEAN search. I learned about that in the early 90s, when the "information highway" was still another word for internet (honest, I actually had a folder named that in my desk drawer, and every time I got a press release about some company with a new website, in that folder it went), and I knew how to do that kind of search. Then, fast forward a decade, and I was watching the TV show "Bones," and the episode in which Jack Hodgins and Bones are buried alive in a car, Zach has to do a Boolean search to discover where they are buried (season one or two, while Zach is still a good guy). But math?

I don't do math. I do words.

@Marlene

Boolean algebra doesn't necessarily resemble what most people think of as "math." To me, math is where you have a group of abstract "things,"  assign basic rules about how those things are going to interact with each other, then study and codify the results of those interactions.

You learned about Boolean operators and how they can be used in a web search, but haven't gone beyond that. All I can say is that you literally don't know what you're missing, and might very well enjoy it.

